  "notes": "One step per hooks.json command registration, in registration order. class: A = per-turn emission, B = action-conditional gate, C = output shaping. impl shim:<path> runs the legacy script unchanged; every step in v1.0.0 is a shim, so no step carries an llm_slot. timeout_ms is the hooks.json timeout in milliseconds; the two entries with no timeout (atom-floor.sh, dispatch-gate.sh) are pinned to 8000. wrapper records the ${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/hooks/lib/sutra-stderr-capture.sh prefix present on that registration. Refreshed 2026-09-12 against sutra 2.264.0 (92 registrations, 87 unique scripts): four steps added at their registration positions — ss.registry-shrink-banner, pre.creation-guard, pre.pytest-isolation-guard, stop.creation-stop-check. deleted[] re-checked against the live hooks/ directory and unchanged at 25: every one of the 22 orphan / cli-lib-test scripts is still registered nowhere, and the 3 inert-stop-cli rows are registered-but-inert by design until the unregistration step. budgets.legacy_wall_ms is the live per-event timeout sum; PreToolUse event_wall_ms raised 150000 -> 170000 to keep the headroom over the new 153000 legacy sum. HOST CAP RECONCILIATION (2026-09-14): the collapsed hooks.json registers ONE command per event, so the host timeout on that registration is the wall for every step folded under it. Each registered timeout is now derived as ceil(max(events[<E>][].timeout_ms)/1000) + 5 s of runtime margin (Stop 155, SessionStart 25, PreToolUse 20, PostToolUse 15, UserPromptSubmit 15, PermissionRequest 8), and event_wall_ms was raised where it sat below that cap: PermissionRequest 5000 -> 8000. spec-check.sh check 12 asserts both directions per event, so raising a step's timeout_ms past its host cap now fails the release gate instead of silently losing the whole event to a SIGKILL. sutra-turn dispatches an event's steps in PARALLEL, mirroring the host, so the real wall of an event is max(step), not sum(step) — which is why the host cap is derived from the largest step budget and not from the sum. event_wall_ms is left ABOVE the cap on the busy events (PreToolUse 170000, Stop 320000): it is the aggregate ceiling check 5 measures the per-event timeout SUM against (153000 / 309000 today), a different quantity from the per-step cap check 12 enforces, and lowering it to the cap would reject sums that are not actually reachable wall-clock. Measured repro (2026-09-14, three parallel 5 s steps with a 15000 ms step budget): alarm at a cap below the step budget -> rc 142, 0 step rows, no ledger file, no stage_digest; alarm at the derived cap -> 3 step rows + emit + stage_digest. EMPTY EVENT DROPPED (2026-09-15): UserPromptExpansion was declared here as an empty step array while the collapsed hooks.json registered `sutra-turn run --event UserPromptExpansion` — a registration hooks.json.step3 never had, so the fleet fires nothing on that path. Every expansion would have forked the runtime to select zero steps, write an orphan_turn + stage_digest row and exit. The event is removed from events, from budgets.event_wall_ms and from budgets.legacy_wall_ms, and from the registry; spec-check check 13 keeps the two sides bound (a declared event must have >= 1 step; a registered runtime event must be declared with >= 1 step), so the pair can never drift back apart. Declaring the event again is a one-line change the day a step exists for it.",
